    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-B1-S1",
      "band": "B1",
      "topic_family": "hybrid_work",
      "text": "A small design company allowed staff to choose two days a week to work from home. Managers expected\nproductivity to rise immediately, but the first month was mixed. Some employees completed individual tasks faster,\nwhile team projects slowed because people were not always available at the same time. The company then\nintroduced shared online hours for collaboration. After that change, project delays fell and most staff wanted the\narrangement to continue.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-B1-01",
        "RD-B1-02"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Article",
      "domain": "occupational",
      "word_count": 71
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-B1-S2",
      "band": "B1",
      "topic_family": "volunteering",
      "text": "Notice A: Volunteers are needed to help visitors find rooms during the town festival. Training is provided on Friday\nevening.\nNotice B: Volunteers are needed to prepare food boxes at the community kitchen. No training is required, but\nvolunteers must be able to lift boxes safely.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-B1-03",
        "RD-B1-04"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Two notices",
      "domain": "public",
      "word_count": 46
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-B1-S3",
      "band": "B1",
      "topic_family": "study_habits",
      "text": "Students often assume that studying for longer always produces better results. In practice, shorter sessions with a\nclear goal can be more effective, especially when learners return to difficult material after a break. This does not\nmean long study sessions are useless; they may be necessary for complex projects. The main point is that time\nalone is a poor measure of useful study.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-B1-05",
        "RD-B1-06"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Short article",
      "domain": "educational",
      "word_count": 63
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-B2-S1",
      "band": "B2",
      "topic_family": "urban_space",
      "text": "A city converted several roadside parking spaces into small public seating areas. Supporters argued that the\nchange made busy streets more pleasant and gave local businesses more foot traffic. Critics worried about parking\nshortages. After six months, surveys showed high use of the seating areas, but the effect on nearby businesses\nvaried greatly by street. The city decided to keep the scheme while collecting more evidence before expanding it.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-B2-01",
        "RD-B2-02"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Article",
      "domain": "public",
      "word_count": 69
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-B2-S2",
      "band": "B2",
      "topic_family": "course_feedback",
      "text": "Many online courses offer flexible access to recorded lessons. [GAP] This flexibility can help learners with irregular\nschedules, but it also places more responsibility on them to plan their own study. Courses that combine recordings\nwith regular check-ins may therefore work better for some learners than fully self-paced programmes.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-B2-03",
        "RD-B2-04"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Paragraph insertion",
      "domain": "educational",
      "word_count": 49
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-B2-S3",
      "band": "B2",
      "topic_family": "reviews",
      "text": "Review 1: The new museum guide is concise and easy to follow, though it sometimes simplifies debates that\ndeserve more explanation.\nReview 2: The guide gives useful background and avoids overwhelming visitors, but people already familiar with the\nsubject may find it too cautious.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-B2-05",
        "RD-B2-06"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Two reviews",
      "domain": "public",
      "word_count": 44
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-C1-S1",
      "band": "C1",
      "topic_family": "public_information",
      "text": "Public information campaigns often aim for simplicity, but simplicity is not the same as clarity. Removing technical\ndetail can make a message easier to read, yet it can also hide uncertainty that matters to a decision. The challenge\nis not to include every available fact. It is to decide which distinctions a reader needs in order to understand both the\nmain message and its limits. A short explanation that signals uncertainty may therefore be more informative than a\nlonger one that presents a single conclusion with unjustified confidence.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-C1-01",
        "RD-C1-02"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Article",
      "domain": "public",
      "word_count": 88
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-C1-S2",
      "band": "C1",
      "topic_family": "research_summary",
      "text": "Abstract A concludes that a short training programme improved participants' confidence, but notes that the study\nhad no comparison group.\nAbstract B reports similar confidence gains in a larger sample and also finds a small improvement on an\nindependent performance task.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-C1-03",
        "RD-C1-04"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Two abstracts",
      "domain": "educational",
      "word_count": 41
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-C1-S3",
      "band": "C1",
      "topic_family": "remote_policy",
      "text": "When organisations introduce a policy across many teams, consistency can be useful: employees know what to\nexpect and managers can apply the same rules. However, uniformity can become misleading when teams perform\nvery different kinds of work. A policy may be formally equal while producing unequal practical effects. The more\nvaried the work, the stronger the case for defining common principles while allowing limited local adaptation.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-C1-05",
        "RD-C1-06"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Article",
      "domain": "occupational",
      "word_count": 66
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-C2-S1",
      "band": "C2",
      "topic_family": "algorithmic_choice",
      "text": "Recommendation systems are often described as tools that reveal what people want. That description is\nincomplete. By deciding which options are made visible, the system also helps shape the preferences it later claims\nmerely to measure. This does not make recommendation inherently manipulative: selection is unavoidable\nwhenever attention is limited. It does, however, complicate the comforting distinction between neutral prediction and\nactive influence. The relevant question is not whether the system influences choice, but whether that influence is\ntransparent, contestable and compatible with the user's interests.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-C2-01",
        "RD-C2-02"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Essay",
      "domain": "public",
      "word_count": 86
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-C2-S2",
      "band": "C2",
      "topic_family": "language_change",
      "text": "Commentary A argues that changes in everyday usage should eventually influence formal dictionaries because\ndictionaries describe a living language.\nCommentary B agrees that usage matters but warns that frequency alone cannot settle questions of meaning,\nregister or appropriateness in every context.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-C2-03",
        "RD-C2-04"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Two commentaries",
      "domain": "educational",
      "word_count": 41
    },
    {
      "stimulus_id": "RD-C2-S3",
      "band": "C2",
      "topic_family": "heritage",
      "text": "Arguments about preserving historic places often turn on the word \"authentic\". Yet authenticity can refer to original\nmaterials, original use, accumulated changes, or the experience a place enables. Restoring a building to an earlier\nstate may increase authenticity in one sense while erasing later history in another. The term is therefore not\nuseless, but it is argumentative: using it without specifying the relevant sense can make a preference appear like a\nneutral fact.",
      "item_ids": [
        "RD-C2-05",
        "RD-C2-06"
      ],
      "stimulus_type": "Essay",
      "domain": "public",
      "word_count": 73
    }
  ],
